<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="trigsub" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Calculus II handout: Trigonometric substitution</title>

      <example>
      <statement><em>(This was Example 1 from the class notes.  The markup language I'm using, "PreTeXt," has opinions about how things are supposed to be numbered.)</em>  Evaluate <m>\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2-25}}\,dx</m>.</statement>
	<solution>
      <p><alert>(The following was written in red on the class notes, which is the color that signifies we are working with respect to <m>\theta</m>.)
	<md>
	   <mrow>{\color{red}{\hbox{Let } x }} \amp {\color{red}{ = 5\sec\theta,}}</mrow>
	   <mrow>{\color{red}{\hbox{ so that } dx }} \amp {\color{red}{ = 5 \sec\theta \tan\theta\ d\theta.}}</mrow>
	</md></alert></p>
<p>(And this was blue in the class notes--a side note that was added after we antidifferentiated the <m>\theta</m>-term and immediately before we drew the right-triangle diagram.)  <em>NOTE that the "Let" statement is actually saying "Let <m>\theta</m> be <m>\sec^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{5}\right)</m>."</em>  And recall that this means that
	<ul>
		<li><m>\frac{x}{5} = \sec\theta</m></li>
		<li>AND (either <m>0 \leq \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m> or <m>\pi \leq \theta \lt \frac{3\pi}{2}</m>).</li>
	</ul>
	</p>
<p><alert>(Back to red ink now, where the markup language will allow it.)  The integral becomes
<md>
<mrow> {\color{red}{\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{(5\sec\theta)^2 - 25}} \cdot 5 \sec\theta \tan\theta\ d\theta }} \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{5^2 \cdot (\sec^2\theta - 1)}} \cdot 5 \sec\theta \tan\theta\ d\theta}} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{5^2} \cdot \sqrt{(\tan\theta)^2}} \cdot 5 \sec\theta \tan\theta\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \sec\theta\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \ln\left|\sec\theta + \tan\theta\right| + C. }} </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>

<p>Note that because of the restrictions on <m>\theta</m>, <m>\tan\theta</m> must be nonnegative, so that <m>\sqrt{(\tan\theta)^2} = \tan\theta</m>.  That was used in the above calculations to replace "<m>\sqrt{(\tan\theta)^2}</m>" with "<m>\tan\theta</m>."</p>

<p>To figure out how to put the antiderivative back into <m>x</m>-language, we use a right-triangle diagram:</p>
<figure xml:id = "figTrigSub01">
    <caption>A right-triangle diagram needed to finish Example 1.</caption>
    <image source = "images/RTDiagramforExample1.jpg" width ="60%">
        <shortdescription>Reference triangles in Quadrants One and Three are labeled so that the secant of the angle theta is x-over-5.</shortdescription>
	<description>Reference triangles for angle theta lie in Quadrants One and Three.  In Quadrant One, the hypotenuse has been labeled as x, the horizontal leg has been labeled as 5, and the resulting value for the vertical leg is the square root of (x-squared minus 25).  In Quadrant Three, the hypotenuse has been labeled as negative-x, the horizontal leg has been labeled as negative-5, and the resulting value for the vertical leg is negative-one times the square root of (x-squared minus 25).</description>
    </image>
</figure>

<p>Now we can finish up by putting the antiderivative formula back into <m>x</m>-language.  We rejoin the calculations above, already in progress:</p>
<p><alert>(We start out in red ink for the "<m>\theta</m>-language" and will switch for the final step when we translate back to "<m>x</m>-language.")  The integral is
<md>
<mrow> {\color{red}{\cdots\ }} \amp {\color{red}{ = \ln\left|\sec\theta + \tan\theta\right| + C }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \ln\left|\frac{x}{5} + \frac{\sqrt{x^2-25}}{5}\right| + C. </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>

<p><alert>A subtle point:</alert>  If you saw this problem in a textbook and looked up the answer in the back of the book, you might see the answer listed as <q><m>\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2-25}}\,dx = \ln\left|x + \sqrt{x^2-25}\right| + C</m>.</q>  And that's not what we obtained!  Were we wrong?  Would that book be wrong?  Neither.  Using our algebra and logarithm-law skills, we note that our answer can be rewritten:  We have
	<md>
	   <mrow>\ln\left|\frac{x}{5} + \frac{\sqrt{x^2-25}}{5}\right| + C \amp = \ln\left|\frac{x + \sqrt{x^2-25}}{5}\right| + C</mrow>
	   <mrow> \amp = \ln\left(\frac{\left|x + \sqrt{x^2-25}\right|}{\left|5\right|}\right) + C</mrow>
	   <mrow> \amp = \ln\left(\left|x + \sqrt{x^2-25}\right|\right) - \ln\left(\left|5\right|\right) + C</mrow>
	   <mrow> \amp = \ln\left(\left|x + \sqrt{x^2-25}\right|\right) + C^*,</mrow>
	</md>
where <m>C^*</m> is a new name for <q><m>-\ln 5 + C</m>.</q>  Do you see what we did there?  That was the <q>arbitrary constant mashup</q>!
</p>
	</solution>
      </example>

    <example>
      <statement><em>(This was Example 2 from the class notes.)</em>  Evaluate <m>\int \frac{\sqrt{9-x^2}}{x^2}\,dx</m>.</statement>
      <solution><p><alert><font color = "FF0000">(The following was written in red on the class notes, which is the color that signifies we are working with respect to <m>\theta</m>.)</font>
	<md>
	   <mrow>{\color{red}{\hbox{Let } x\ }} \amp {\color{red}{ = 3\sin\theta,}} </mrow>
	   <mrow>{\color{red}{\hbox{ so that } dx\ }} \amp {\color{red}{ = 3 \cos\theta\ d\theta.}} </mrow>
	</md></alert></p>
<p>(And this was blue in the class notes--a side note that was added after we antidifferentiated the <m>\theta</m>-term and immediately before we drew the right-triangle diagram.)  <em>NOTE that the "Let" statement is actually saying "Let <m>\theta</m> be <m>\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{3}\right)</m>."</em>  And recall that this means that
	<ul>
		<li><m>\frac{x}{3} = \sin\theta</m></li>
		<li>AND <m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \leq \theta \leq \frac{\pi}{2}</m>.</li>
	</ul>
	</p>
<p><alert>(Back to red ink now, wherever the markup language will allow it.)  The integral becomes
<md>
<mrow> {\color{red}{\int \frac{\sqrt{9-9\sin^2\theta}}{9\sin^2\theta} \cdot 3 \cos\theta\ d\theta }} \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{\sqrt{9 \cdot (1 - \sin^2\theta)}}{9\sin^2\theta} \cdot 3 \cos\theta\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{\sqrt{9} \cdot \sqrt{\cos^2\theta}}{9\sin^2\theta} \cdot 3 \cos\theta\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{\cos^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \left(\frac{\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\right)^2\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \cot^2\theta\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int (\csc^2\theta - 1)\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = -\cot\theta - \theta + C. }} </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>

<p>(Blue ink again--a side note.)  Note that because of the restrictions on <m>\theta</m>, <m>\cos\theta</m> must be nonnegative, so that <m>\sqrt{(\cos\theta)^2} = \cos\theta</m>.  That was used in the above calculations to replace "<m>\sqrt{(\cos\theta)^2}</m>" with "<m>\cos\theta</m>."</p>

<p>To figure out how to put the antiderivative back into <m>x</m>-language, we use a right-triangle diagram:</p>

<figure xml:id = "figTrigSub02">
    <caption>A right-triangle diagram needed to finish Example 2.</caption>
    <image source = "images/RTDiagramforExample2.jpg" width ="60%">
        <shortdescription>Reference triangles in Quadrants One and Four are labeled so that the sine of the angle theta is x-over-3.</shortdescription>
	<description>Reference triangles for angle theta lie in Quadrants One and Four.  In each Quadrant, the hypotenuse has been labeled as 3, the vertical leg has been labeled as x, and the resulting value for the horizontal leg is the square root of (9 minus x-squared).  It is noted that in each Quadrant, the cotangent of theta is (the square root of (9 minus x-squared)), over x.</description>
    </image>
</figure>

<p>Now we can finish up by putting the antiderivative formula back into <m>x</m>-language.  We rejoin the calculations above, already in progress:</p>
<p><alert>(We start out in red ink for the "<m>\theta</m>-language" and will switch for the final step when we translate back to "<m>x</m>-language.")  The integral is
<md>
<mrow> {\color{red}{\cdots\ }} \amp {\color{red}{ = -\cot\theta - \theta + C }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = -\frac{\sqrt{9-x^2}}{x} - \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{3}\right) + C. </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>
	</solution>
      </example>

    <example>
      <statement><em>(This was Example 3 from the class notes.)</em>  Evaluate <m>\int \frac{1}{(x^2-6x+11)^2}\,dx</m>.</statement>

	<solution>
	<p>Note first that <m>x^2-6x+11=(x-3)^2+2</m>. Then the integral can be written as
<md> \int\ \frac{1}{((x-3)^2+2)^2}\ dx.</md></p>
      <p><alert>(The following was written in red on the class notes, which is the color that signifies we are working with respect to <m>\theta</m>.  PreTeXt, the system used to typeset these notes, does not allow changing the color of a font.)
	<md>
	   <mrow>{\color{red}{\hbox{Let } x-3 }} \amp {\color{red}{ = \sqrt{2}\tan\theta,}}</mrow>
	   <mrow>{\color{red}{\hbox{ so that } dx }} \amp {\color{red}{ = \sqrt{2} \sec^2\theta\ d\theta.}}</mrow>
	</md></alert></p>
<p>(And this was blue in the class notes--a side note that was added after we antidifferentiated the <m>\theta</m>-term and immediately before we drew the right-triangle diagram.)  <em>NOTE that the "Let" statement is actually saying "Let <m>\theta</m> be <m>\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{x-3}{\sqrt{2}}\right)</m>."</em>  And recall that this means that
	<ul>
		<li><m>\frac{x-3}{\sqrt{2}} = \tan\theta</m></li>
		<li>AND <m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m>.</li>
	</ul>
	</p>
<p><alert>(Back to red ink now, where the markup language will allow it.)  The integral becomes
<md>
<mrow> {\color{red}{\int \frac{1}{((\sqrt{2} \cdot \tan\theta)^2+2)^2} \cdot \sqrt{2} \sec^2\theta\ d\theta }} \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{1}{(2 \cdot \tan^2\theta+2)^2} \cdot \sqrt{2} \sec^2\theta\ d\theta }}  </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{1}{(2 \cdot (\tan^2\theta+1))^2} \cdot \sqrt{2} \sec^2\theta\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{1}{4 \cdot \sec^4\theta} \cdot \sqrt{2} \sec^2\theta\ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \int \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}} \cdot \frac{1}{\sec^2\theta} \ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}} \cdot \int \cos^2\theta \ d\theta }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}} \cdot \left(\frac{1}{2}\theta - \frac{1}{4}\sin(2\theta) + C_0\right) }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp {\color{red}{ = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}} \cdot \left(\frac{1}{2}\theta - \frac{1}{4} \cdot 2\sin\theta\cos\theta + C_0\right) }} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{4\sqrt{2}}\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{x-3}{\sqrt{2}}\right) + \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}} \cdot \frac{x-3}{\sqrt{(x-3)^2+2}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{(x-3)^2+2}} + C </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{4\sqrt{2}}\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{x-3}{\sqrt{2}}\right) + \frac{x-3}{2\sqrt{x^2-6x+11}} + C. </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>

<p>(Blue ink again--a side note.)  Note that because of the restrictions on <m>\theta</m>, <m>\sec\theta</m> must be nonnegative, so that <m>\sqrt{(\sec\theta)^2} = \sec\theta</m>.  That was used in the above calculations to replace "<m>\sqrt{(\sec\theta)^2}</m>" with "<m>\sec\theta</m>."  Below is the diagram that helped us put the antiderivative back into <m>x</m>-language.</p>

    <figure xml:id = "figTrigSub03">
       <caption>A right-triangle diagram needed to finish Example 3.</caption>
      <image source = "images/RTDiagramforExample3.jpg" width ="60%">
          <shortdescription>Reference triangles in Quadrants One and Four are labeled so that the tangent of the angle theta is (x minus 3)-over-(the square root of 2).</shortdescription>
	        <description>Reference triangles for angle theta lie in Quadrants One and Four.  In each Quadrant, the vertical leg has been labeled as x minus 3, the horizontal leg has been labeled as the square root of 2, and the resulting value for the hypotenuse is the square root of ((x minus 3)-squared plus (the square root of 2)-squared).</description>
      </image>
    </figure>

	</solution>
      </example>

    <example>
      <statement><em>(These were Examples 4 to 7 from the class notes.)</em>  Find the <q>milk equation</q> for each integral. You may need to <q>almost complete the square</q> to get a quadratic expression into one of the three appropriate forms.

        <ul>
         <li>(a) <m>\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+4x^2}}\,dx</m>: Let <m>2x = \tan\theta</m>.  (<q>Then <m>2\,dx = \sec^2\theta\,d\theta.\,\ldots\,</m></q>)</li>
         <li>(b) <m>\int \frac{1}{x^2\sqrt{16-x^2}}\,dx</m>: Let <m>x = 4\sin\theta</m>.  (<q>Then <m>dx = 4\cos\theta\,d\theta.\,\ldots\,</m></q>)</li>
         <li>(c) <m>\int \sqrt{x^2-9}\,dx</m>: Let <m>x = 3\sec\theta</m>.  (<q>Then <m>dx = 3\sec\theta \tan\theta\,d\theta.\,\ldots\,</m></q>)</li>
         <li>(d) <m>\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{9x^2+6x+2}}\,dx</m>: Note first that <m>9x^2+6x+2 = (3x + 1)^2 + 1</m>.  Let <m>3x + 1 = 1 \cdot \tan\theta</m>.  (<q>Then <m>3\,dx = \sec^2\theta\,d\theta.\,\ldots\,</m></q>)</li>
        </ul>
        </statement>
    </example>

</section>